## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of maintaining an ordered dynamic sequence with logarithmic updates while augmenting nodes with aggregate data for fast queries.

## Key Concepts
- Implicit treap for sequence indexing
- Split by position and merge operations
- Augmented data: size and sum to support rangeSum
- Handling duplicates via node values and randomized priorities

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node { constructor(val){ this.val=val; this.pri=Math.random(); this.left=null; this.right=null; this.sz=1; this.sum=val; } }
function sz(n){ return n? n.sz:0; }
function sum(n){ return n? n.sum:0; }
function upd(n){ if(n){ n.sz = 1 + sz(n.left) + sz(n.right); n.sum = n.val + sum(n.left) + sum(n.right); } }
function split(root, key){ // first key elements go to left
  if(!root) return [null,null];
  if(sz(root.left) >= key){ const [l,r] = split(root.left, key); root.left = r; upd(root); return [l, root]; }
  else { const [l,r] = split(root.right, key - sz(root.left) - 1); root.right = l; upd(root); return [root, r]; }
}
function merge(a,b){ if(!a||!b) return a||b; if(a.pri < b.pri){ a.right = merge(a.right, b); upd(a); return a; } else { b.left = merge(a, b.left); upd(b); return b; } }
function insertAt(root, index, value){ const node = new Node(value); const [L,R] = split(root, index); return merge(merge(L, node), R); }
function eraseAt(root, index){ const [L, R] = split(root, index); const [M, RR] = split(R, 1); return merge(L, RR); }
function rangeSumQuery(root, l, r){ const [A, BR] = split(root, l); const [B, C] = split(BR, r - l + 1); const res = sum(B); const newRoot = merge(A, merge(B, C)); return {root: newRoot, sum: res}; }
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates proficiency in interval stabbing, weighted multisets, and persistent versioning within a single challenge, while also testing the ability to handle overlapping intervals and duplicates elegantly.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over coordinates** for efficient interval partitioning with O(log n) node coverage per operation
- **Per-node multisets** implemented as order-statistic trees enabling duplicate-aware weight tracking and rank queries
- **Path-copying persistence** to maintain an immutable version history with O(log n) space overhead per update
- **Multiset semantics** for handling duplicate intervals through count-based insertions and deletions

## Code Example
```javascript
class IntervalMultiset {
  constructor() {
    this.roots = new Map(); // version -> segment tree root
    this.roots.set(0, null);
    this.currentVersion = 0;
  }

  insertInterval(l, r, w) {
    this.currentVersion++;
    const newRoot = insert(this.roots.get(this.currentVersion - 1), l, r, w);
    this.roots.set(this.currentVersion, newRoot);
  }

  eraseInterval(l, r, w) {
    this.currentVersion++;
    const newRoot = erase(this.roots.get(this.currentVersion - 1), l, r, w);
    this.roots.set(this.currentVersion, newRoot);
  }

  sumWeightsCoveringPoint(p, t) {
    return querySum(this.roots.get(t), p);
  }

  kthLargestWeightCoveringPoint(p, t, k) {
    return queryKth(this.roots.get(t), p, k);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| insertInterval | O(log² n) | O(log n) new nodes |
| eraseInterval | O(log² n) | O(log n) new nodes |
| sumWeightsCoveringPoint | O(log n) | O(1) |
| kthLargestWeightCoveringPoint | O(log² n) | O(1) |
